-C014,PDF p.10 | 3. Theory of Change | table 5 block 160,28% points,28%,none,CITED,src\imported\data_layer_expansion.py fetch_afrobarometer_data() (hand-entered Round 8 figures) -> data\external\afrobarometer_summary.json,NEEDS_CITATION,W10,59.8% is a composite with assumed weights (30/35/20/15); the 28 pp gap = 71% civic duty - 43% government listens. Components can be CITED; the composite needs W10 decision. [row: KEY INSIGHT | Tanzanians already demonstrate substantial tax morale. The challenge is converting that willingness i],,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,"Recomputed from the components: civic duty (Q76e-tan, always) minus 'government listens' = 42.6 pp (95% CI 39.1 to 46.1) with local councillors (Q38b) and 50.5 pp (47.5 to 53.5) with MPs (Q38a); against 'taxes are used for citizens' well-being' (Q47c) the difference is -10.8 pp. The report's 71% - 43% = 28 pp cannot be reproduced from any item in the file.",COMPUTED (survey data),"src/03_features/afrobarometer_morale.py -> data/processed/afrobarometer_morale.json, outputs/tables/tax_morale_components.csv",RECOMPUTED - TEXT TO CHANGE,The gap depends on which reciprocity item is used; state the variables.,"Two gaps and a contrast, each with its variables: civic duty (Q76E_TAN 'always' 65.7%) minus councillors listening (Q38B often/always 23.1%) = 42.6 pp (95% CI 39.1 to 46.1); minus MPs listening (Q38A often/always 15.2%) = 50.5 pp (47.5 to 53.5); use-of-revenue contrast: civic duty minus 'the government usually uses the tax revenues it collects for the well-being of citizens' (Q47C agree/strongly agree 76.5%) = -10.8 pp (-13.9 to -7.7). The report's 71% - 43% = 28 pp is not reproducible.",COMPUTED (survey data),src/03_features/afrobarometer_morale.py -> data/processed/afrobarometer_morale.json,RECOMPUTED - TEXT TO CHANGE,"E2.0: both listening gaps and the use-of-revenue contrast are recorded, with their variables."
